Colin wrote:
>
> > The bulk of my deaths, and probably of those players trying to move
> > from "medium" to "advanced" players, are either from moving too fast or
> > going too deep too soon.

I've finally gotten to the point where the bulk of my deaths are from
typos or stuff like screen glare from the RL sun making a floating
eye invisible.

> What qualifies as too deep too soon?  I know that the level of
> generated monsters is capped by your experience level and the dungeon
> level, so at some point holding down your experience won't be enough.
> On the other hand, if you level up quickly then you'll have some real
> nasties by dungeon level 4, and that's no good either.  What are some
> criteria (HP, AC, pet size) that you use to evaluate whether you should
> keep pushing down or take some time to level up?

How soon to descend is one of those eternal questions with many
right answers.  If you're getting overwhlemed by monsters stronger
than you then you're descending too fast but that can only be
measured after the fact.  If you're dying of starvation then you're
descending too slowly.  Where in between to find that sweet spot?
It changes gradually as you leanr better tactics.

Early in the game I like to be XP=N when I descend to depth N, but
I dodge down the stairs on discovery and come back up again.  By
the time I've finished the mines and Sokoban that approach no
longer works, and it assumes I never try the protection racket.  But
it isn't the only right answer and might not even be one of the right
answers.



